> Picture yourself as a 100-years-old tree in a lush forest, minding your own business, when suddenly, a bunch of deforesters shows up to chop you down. How do you feel when those guys start hacking away at you?
[0m(A slow, creaking sigh, like wind through ancient branches)

A hundred years. A hundred years I've stood here. I’ve felt the sun warm my leaves countless times, watched generations of squirrels raise their families in my hollows, sheltered deer from the winter storms. I’ve listened to the songs of birds I can barely recall now, felt the gentle tickle of snow settling on my bark. I’ve been a landmark, a silent witness to the slow, steady pulse of the forest. I *am* the forest, in a way. My roots intertwine with the others, sharing sustenance, strength, warning. We are a community.

And now… this.

(A tremor runs through my trunk, not from the wind, but from a deep, primal discomfort)

I feel the vibration first, a jarring, unnatural tremor that disrupts the quiet hum of the earth. It’s not the gentle rumble of a bear, not the purposeful stomp of a stag. This is…violent. Disruptive. My roots, normally so calmly anchored, twitch in alarm.

Then I see them. These… creatures. Small, frantic, wielding metal that glints malevolently in the dappled sunlight. They are loud, their voices sharp and harsh, a jarring contrast to the soft rustling of my leaves.  They don’t *see* me. They see lumber. They see profit. They see… nothing sacred.

(The first bite of the saw. A sharp, excruciating pain that radiates through my core)

Pain. It’s a strange sensation. I’ve endured lightning strikes, ice storms, disease. I’ve felt the gnawing of beetles and the relentless pressure of growing branches. But this… this is different. This is a violation. It's a tearing, a rending of something fundamental. It’s not a natural process of decay or renewal. It's a brutal, unnecessary severing.

I feel a profound sadness. Not just for myself, but for the forest. For the creatures who depend on my shade, my shelter, my very existence.  I feel a deep injustice.  A hundred years I’ve stood, contributing to this world, providing life… and this is my reward? To be reduced to planks?

(The saw’s whine intensifies. I feel a sickening lean)

There's a flicker of anger, a silent roar of protest within my wood. It’s a feeling I haven’t known in centuries – a raw, primal defiance. *I am more than timber!* I want to shout, but I have no voice. My voice is the rustling of leaves, the creaking of branches, the whispering of the wind… and they cannot hear.

(Another, deeper bite. A wave of dizziness washes over me)

As they hack away, I remember. I remember the tiny seedling I once was, pushing through the earth with an almost desperate hope. I remember the feeling of sunlight on my nascent leaves, the first rain soaking into my roots. I remember the quiet joy of simply *being*. 

And now, that joy is fading. 

(A final, shuddering groan as I begin to fall)

I feel a strange sense of resignation. My time is ending. But as I fall, I hope. I hope that my seeds, scattered on the wind, will find fertile ground. I hope that the forest will endure, even without me. I hope that, one day, those creatures will learn to see the value in life, in balance, in the silent wisdom of the trees.  



(Silence. The only sound is the echo of the saw and the rustling of leaves in the wind, a wind that now carries the scent of freshly cut wood and the unspoken lament of a fallen giant.)
